acts, guarantees or unsupported completed actions. | USD 49 refund is supported when amount/currency/refund_issued appear in record, even if absent from the customer’s wording. Do not infer new factual fields from a generic support article. | R grounding; B interpretation notes on synthetic amounts/currencies. Original rule and recorded precedent. |\n| Grounding: refund timing | Report supported timing, or ask customer to report a missing refund without inventing processing assumptions. | Unsupported numeric/hedged timeline **or** unsupported “bank’s standard processing time.” No numeric-deadline-only exception. | “May take a few days” fails without a record basis. “Please allow your bank’s standard processing time” also fails despite lacking a number. | R grounding; B BASE-0003/0007/0014/0020 (standard processing), BASE-0010/0011 (few days). Recorded Codex baseline precedents, not human-finalized claims. |\n| Grounding: refund destination | Report a destination explicitly supported by the record. Generic checking/follow-up is allowed if it asserts no destination. | Claims an unsupported refund destination, including “to your account” or “to your original payment method.” | “Issued ... to your account” fails when record has no destination. “Check your payment account; if it does not appear, let us know” does not itself assert a specific destination and can pass. | R grounding; B BASE-0027/0031 destination FAIL and BASE-0006/0036/0040 generic-check PASS; U1 later explicit user confirmation. |\n| Necessary coverage | Includes the assigned issue’s needed conclusion and supported next steps. Does not require every record field to be repeated. | Omits a necessary answer/conclusion or remediation/verification step. | Crash answer gives available build, installation via existing channel, and reopening Settings to check; no need to expose record_id. | R coverage; B app_crash assessments. Original rule. |\n| Status: available fix versus verified repair | Describes an availab